ished by their cytokine profiles and phenotypic markers. <source>J. Immunol.</source> <volume>164</volume>:<fpage>208</fpage>&#x02013;216.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10605013</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib14"><label>14</label><citation citation-type="journal">Seewaldt, S., H.E. Thomas, M. Ejrnaes, U. Christen, T. Wolfe, E. Rodrigo, B. Coon, B. Michelsen, T.W. Kay, and M.G. von Herrath. <year>2000</year>. Virus-induced autoimmune diabetes: most beta-cells die through inflammatory cytokines and not perforin from autoreactive (anti-viral) cytotoxic T-lymphocytes. <source>Diabetes.</source> <volume>49</volume>:<fpage>1801</fpage>&#x02013;1809.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11078446</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib15"><label>15</label><citation citation-type="journal">Amrani, A., J. Verdaguer, B. Anderson, T. Utsugi, S. Bou, and P. Santamaria. <year>1999</year>. Perforin-independent beta-cell destruction by diabetogenic CD8(+) T lymphocytes in transgenic nonobese diabetic mice. <source>J. Clin. Invest.</source> <volume>103</volume>:<fpage>1201</fpage>&#x02013;1209.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">10207172</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib16"><label>16</label><citation citation-type="journal">Liblau, R.S., F.S. Wong, L.T. Mars, and P. Santamaria. <year>2002</year>. Autoreactive CD8 T cells in organ-specific autoimmunity: emerging targets for therapeutic intervention. <source>Immunity.</source> <volume>17</volume>:<fpage>1</fpage>&#x02013;6.<pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">12150886</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib17"><label>17</label><citation citation-type="journal">DiLorenzo, T.P., R.T. Graser, T. Ono, G.J. Christianson, H.D. Chapman, D.C. Roopenian, S.G. Nathenson, and D.V. Serreze. <year>1998</year>. Major histocompatibility complex class I-restricted T cells are required for all but the end stages of diabetes development in nonobese diabetic mice and use a prevalent T cell receptor alpha chain gene rearrangement. <source>Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA.</source> <volum